  "coverage_qualification": [
    "The derivative-support hypothesis is imposed on the actual continuous real dual of the original test space, with the actual negative-transpose coordinate derivatives.",
    "The independently specified inward loci and fixed-centre components exist and are sealed. Their original set-distance characterization holds for a nonempty complement; positive ball margins retain an empty complement without assigning it a finite distance.",
    "Every compact support is eventually captured by the original inner components, by the earlier compact finite-cover and directed finite-upper-bound theorems.",
    "The actual norm-smooth dual density has zero coordinate derivatives there. The previous rank-zero theorem gives connected constancy; no positive dimension or whole-target separability is assumed.",
    "The fixed original normalized kernel test has vector integral y. Uniqueness of its positive scalar limit proves that the actual small-scale dual constants agree.",
    "The compact cutoff restriction is inverse to zero extension on the original plateau and commutes there with actual test derivatives. Relatively compact connected subdomains and unit tests on overlaps give the original arbitrary-open-domain conclusion.",
    "The final statement retains the original test space D(U,Y) and literal scalar subset integral over U. Empty A is handled by the zero test and zero continuous functional.",
    "Later items 4.1.5\u20134.1.34 remain required, including 4.1.33 and 4.1.34; pure historical remarks and external pointers are excluded by user scope."
  ],
